1
00:00:01,200 --> 00:00:09,400
Two crews started at opposite ends of a
mile of solid sandstone, and they dug
toward each other in the dark.

2
00:00:09,800 --> 00:00:13,556
They used a hundred and forty-six tons of
dynamite.

3
00:00:13,606 --> 00:00:18,258
There was no GPS and no laser, only
surveyors, string and math.

4
00:00:18,308 --> 00:00:28,780
And when the two bores finally broke
through into each other, a newspaper
reported that nowhere were they more than
three inches out of line.

5
00:00:29,170 --> 00:00:41,670
It is the Zion–Mount Carmel tunnel, and to
understand why anyone would try to build
it, you have to start with a painter who
showed the world a canyon it did not
believe.

6
00:00:42,080 --> 00:00:46,581
This is America's National Parks: The
Hidden History.

7
00:00:46,631 --> 00:00:48,041
Zion, Part Three.

8
00:00:48,091 --> 00:00:49,930
The Impossible Tunnel.

9
00:00:54,330 --> 00:01:02,259
For most of the nineteenth century, Zion
Canyon was known mostly to the farming
towns of southern Utah.

10
00:01:02,309 --> 00:01:05,280
To most of the country, it was a rumor.

11
00:01:05,680 --> 00:01:11,961
In the summer of nineteen oh three, a
painter named Frederick Dellenbaugh came
to see it.

12
00:01:12,011 --> 00:01:14,166
He was no stranger to the West.

13
00:01:14,216 --> 00:01:20,497
As a young man he had traveled with John
Wesley Powell, and he had seen a lot of
canyons.

14
00:01:20,547 --> 00:01:21,991
This one stopped him.

15
00:01:22,041 --> 00:01:27,610
In his words, never before had such a
naked mountain of rock entered our minds.

16
00:01:28,000 --> 00:01:28,986
He painted it.

17
00:01:29,036 --> 00:01:34,539
His canvases of Zion were shown at the
nineteen oh four World's Fair in St.

18
00:01:34,589 --> 00:01:41,720
Louis, and he wrote about the canyon for
Scribner's Magazine, calling it a new
valley of wonders.

19
00:01:42,130 --> 00:01:47,830
According to the National Park Service,
the visitors who saw those paintings in
St.

20
00:01:47,880 --> 00:01:51,363
Louis doubted that a place like that could
be real.

21
00:01:51,413 --> 00:01:53,372
It was a hard thing to prove.

22
00:01:53,422 --> 00:01:59,330
There was no highway to the canyon, and
for most people, the paint was the only
proof.

23
00:02:03,720 --> 00:02:10,420
Back in Utah, people who actually lived
near it started asking for official
protection.

24
00:02:10,820 --> 00:02:17,441
In nineteen oh eight, eight ranchers from
southern Utah asked the government for a
land survey.

25
00:02:17,491 --> 00:02:21,304
The General Land Office sent a surveyor
named Leo Snow.

26
00:02:21,354 --> 00:02:29,731
He surveyed the canyon and wrote that it
was surpassed in grandeur only by the view
of the Grand Canyon of the Colorado.

27
00:02:29,781 --> 00:02:35,700
His recommendation was short: Zion Canyon,
he said, ought to be made a national park.

28
00:02:36,100 --> 00:02:46,537
On July thirty-first, nineteen oh nine,
President William Howard Taft signed a
proclamation that created Mukuntuweap
National Monument.

29
00:02:46,587 --> 00:02:54,150
It was small, about fifteen thousand
acres, a strip roughly half a mile wide
and eight miles long.

30
00:02:54,550 --> 00:02:56,167
The name didn't last.

31
00:02:56,217 --> 00:03:07,600
It was unpopular with local people, and in
nineteen eighteen the monument was renamed
Zion and enlarged to more than seventy-six
thousand acres.

32
00:03:08,000 --> 00:03:18,450
And on November nineteenth, nineteen
nineteen, Congress made Zion a national
park, Utah's first, signed by President
Woodrow Wilson.

33
00:03:18,850 --> 00:03:21,416
But a park on paper is not a park.

34
00:03:21,466 --> 00:03:26,572
In nineteen twenty, about thirty-seven
hundred people visited Zion.

35
00:03:26,622 --> 00:03:32,420
There was a dirt road, a tent camp, and no
way to get here that wasn't hard.

36
00:03:36,820 --> 00:03:41,013
Some of the people who saw the opportunity
were railroad men.

37
00:03:41,063 --> 00:03:49,083
Union Pacific and its subsidiary, the Utah
Parks Company, wanted tourists to ride
their trains to the western parks.

38
00:03:49,133 --> 00:04:02,300
A rail spur reached Cedar City, Utah, in
nineteen twenty-three, and one historian
counts about one point seven million
dollars spent on improvements in nineteen
twenty-three and twenty-four.

39
00:04:02,700 --> 00:04:07,138
In June nineteen twenty-three, the biggest
tourist of all arrived.

40
00:04:07,188 --> 00:04:13,461
President Warren Harding came to Cedar
City, Utah, by train, to thousands of
cheering locals.

41
00:04:13,511 --> 00:04:18,357
Sheriffs rounded up about seventy-five
horses to ride him up the canyon.

42
00:04:18,407 --> 00:04:25,904
According to a Deseret News account, the
president wore a cowboy hat, a kerchief
and a pair of oversized chaps.

43
00:04:25,954 --> 00:04:30,800
His wife, Florence, said she wouldn't have
missed the trip for anything.

44
00:04:31,200 --> 00:04:33,982
Five weeks later, Harding was dead.

45
00:04:34,032 --> 00:04:38,917
He died in San Francisco on August second,
still on the trip.

46
00:04:38,967 --> 00:04:41,506
But the visit had done its work.

47
00:04:41,556 --> 00:04:42,800
Zion was famous.

48
00:04:43,200 --> 00:04:45,933
Visitors came by train and by bus.

49
00:04:45,983 --> 00:04:55,920
By nineteen thirty, nearly fifty-five
thousand people visited in a year, about
fifteen times as many as ten years
earlier.

50
00:04:56,320 --> 00:04:59,420
And that created a new problem.

51
00:05:03,820 --> 00:05:06,214
Zion Canyon is a box canyon.

52
00:05:06,264 --> 00:05:10,840
The road went in, and the road came out
the same way.

53
00:05:11,250 --> 00:05:17,606
Bryce Canyon and the other wonders of the
plateau country lay beyond the cliff wall.

54
00:05:17,656 --> 00:05:24,850
To drive from Zion to Bryce, you had to go
the long way around, a hundred and forty-
nine miles.

55
00:05:25,250 --> 00:05:30,552
There was an older way over that wall, and
you have already heard about it.

56
00:05:30,602 --> 00:05:40,828
Around nineteen hundred, a logger named
David Flanigan strung about fifty thousand
feet of wire up the east rim to lower
lumber into the canyon.

57
00:05:40,878 --> 00:05:46,180
Before that, a wagon trip to bring lumber
down could take a week or longer.

58
00:05:46,570 --> 00:05:55,290
And according to the Park Service, a
family dog named Darkey once rode that
wire two thousand feet down the mountain.

59
00:05:55,700 --> 00:05:57,529
A dog could do it.

60
00:05:57,579 --> 00:05:59,200
A car could not.

61
00:06:03,600 --> 00:06:18,670
So the Park Service, the state of Utah and
the Bureau of Public Roads agreed to build
something that sounded almost absurd: a
road up the side of the canyon, and a
tunnel through the cliff above it, to the
plateau.

62
00:06:19,070 --> 00:06:23,283
It took more than four years of surveys
just to find a route.

63
00:06:23,333 --> 00:06:29,713
A local stockman, John Winder, is credited
with showing the surveyors the way up Pine
Creek.

64
00:06:29,763 --> 00:06:37,470
When they were done, the road to Bryce
Canyon would shrink from a hundred and
forty-nine miles to eighty-eight.

65
00:06:37,870 --> 00:06:41,782
Construction began in September nineteen
twenty-seven.

66
00:06:41,832 --> 00:06:45,817
A Nevada construction company from Fallon
took the job.

67
00:06:45,867 --> 00:06:51,320
About two hundred workers and their
families lived in a camp near the work.

68
00:06:51,720 --> 00:06:57,821
The crews started by blasting openings in
the cliff face, windows high above Pine
Creek.

69
00:06:57,871 --> 00:07:02,295
Through them, they hauled out the rock and
let in air and light.

70
00:07:02,345 --> 00:07:09,984
Then they bored the tunnel in two
directions, toward each other, and widened
it with dynamite and air shovels.

71
00:07:10,034 --> 00:07:12,570
Several times, parts of it collapsed.

72
00:07:12,980 --> 00:07:14,712
The work was dangerous.

73
00:07:14,762 --> 00:07:22,457
One local history names two men who died:
a crew boss, Johnny Morrison, and a man
named Mac McClain.

74
00:07:22,507 --> 00:07:31,830
The Park Service pages don't mention
deaths, so we say it carefully: at least
two men reportedly died building this
road.

75
00:07:32,220 --> 00:07:39,470
When the two bores met, a newspaper
reported, they were nowhere more than
three inches out of line.

76
00:07:39,520 --> 00:07:43,820
After a mile of rock, that was close
enough to shake hands.

77
00:07:48,220 --> 00:07:52,378
The tunnel was dedicated on July fourth,
nineteen thirty.

78
00:07:52,428 --> 00:07:55,331
Utah's governor, George Dern, was there.

79
00:07:55,381 --> 00:08:02,565
A prayer was offered by the president of
the Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day
Saints, Heber J.

80
00:08:02,615 --> 00:08:03,008
Grant.

81
00:08:03,058 --> 00:08:11,350
Visiting governors from a governors'
conference came to see it, and the
ceremony was broadcast live on the radio.

82
00:08:11,750 --> 00:08:20,553
By the numbers, it was one point one mile
long, twenty-two feet wide and sixteen
feet high, with six windows cut into the
cliff.

83
00:08:20,603 --> 00:08:24,358
The tunnel alone had cost about half a
million dollars.

84
00:08:24,408 --> 00:08:30,098
The whole highway cost closer to two
million, at the start of the Great
Depression.

85
00:08:30,148 --> 00:08:37,430
At the time, the National Park Service
called it the longest vehicular tunnel in
the national park system.

86
00:08:37,830 --> 00:08:38,580
It worked.

87
00:08:38,630 --> 00:08:40,980
Zion was no longer a dead end.

88
00:08:41,030 --> 00:08:46,100
Before the tunnel, the park had about a
thousand visitors a day.

89
00:08:46,150 --> 00:08:48,580
It was about to see a lot more.

90
00:08:52,980 --> 00:08:56,868
The tunnel is still there, and it is still
in use.

91
00:08:56,918 --> 00:09:05,610
In two thousand twelve, the American
Society of Civil Engineers named it a
historic civil engineering landmark.

92
00:09:06,000 --> 00:09:07,769
Not everything has lasted.

93
00:09:07,819 --> 00:09:12,597
One of the windows was closed because
seepage turned the road to ice.

94
00:09:12,647 --> 00:09:18,895
Another was sealed by a rockslide in
nineteen fifty-eight, and two more were
closed later.

95
00:09:18,945 --> 00:09:29,880
The same mountain that the tunnel crosses
keeps working on it, which is the same
story as the lakebed, the lodge and the
trail: this canyon is always moving.

96
00:09:30,270 --> 00:09:39,176
And because it was built for nineteen-
twenties cars, today's biggest vehicles
can't squeeze through alongside traffic.

97
00:09:39,226 --> 00:09:47,600
Anything over eleven feet four inches
tall, or seven feet ten wide, needs a
permit and one-way traffic control.

98
00:09:52,000 --> 00:09:59,493
Today, Zion sees nearly five million
visits a year, the second most visited
national park in America.

99
00:09:59,543 --> 00:10:06,289
Every one of them is looking at something
a painter once swore was real, and a crowd
in St.

100
00:10:06,339 --> 00:10:08,530
Louis could not quite believe.

101
00:10:08,920 --> 00:10:20,937
From a mountain that fell in a minute, to
a lake that filled itself in, to a tunnel
that met in the middle, Zion has always
been a place that asks a lot of the people
who love it.

102
00:10:20,987 --> 00:10:24,240
And somehow it keeps giving them paradise
anyway.

103
00:10:24,650 --> 00:10:31,700
If you've stayed with us this far, the
full Zion film puts all three parts
together in one watch.

104
00:10:31,750 --> 00:10:33,530
See you in the next park.
